{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "name": "Mass assignment of role at registration allows unauthenticated admin account creation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Mass assignment of role at registration allows unauthenticated admin account creation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The user registration endpoint binds the client-supplied `role` field without restriction, allowing any unauthenticated attacker to create an account with the `admin` role and receive a valid admin JWT on login."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The user registration endpoint binds the client-supplied `role` field without restriction, allowing any unauthenticated attacker to create an account with the `admin` role and receive a valid admin JWT on login.\n\nImmediate full administrator compromise of the application from an unauthenticated position: the attacker can list all user accounts and their data, read/modify any user's basket and orders, modify products and prices, view accounting data, and access all admin functionality. No prior credentials or user interaction required.\n\nRemove the `role` field from the request payload bound at registration and hard-code the default role server-side; maintain an explicit field allowlist for user creation so client-supplied privilege, status, or entitlement fields (role, isActive, deluxeToken) are never persisted. Reject or silently discard any attempt to set a privileged role at the public registration endpoint.",
                "markdown": "The user registration endpoint binds the client-supplied `role` field without restriction, allowing any unauthenticated attacker to create an account with the `admin` role and receive a valid admin JWT on login.\n\nImmediate full administrator compromise of the application from an unauthenticated position: the attacker can list all user accounts and their data, read/modify any user's basket and orders, modify products and prices, view accounting data, and access all admin functionality. No prior credentials or user interaction required.\n\nRemove the `role` field from the request payload bound at registration and hard-code the default role server-side; maintain an explicit field allowlist for user creation so client-supplied privilege, status, or entitlement fields (role, isActive, deluxeToken) are never persisted. Reject or silently discard any attempt to set a privileged role at the public registration endpoint."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.4",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0002",
              "name": "Authentication bypass via unsigned JWT with alg:none (arbitrary identity forgery including admin)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Authentication bypass via unsigned JWT with alg:none (arbitrary identity forgery including admin)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The backend accepts JWTs with algorithm `none` and an empty signature, allowing any unauthenticated attacker to mint a token for an arbitrary identity — including the `admin` role — and receive full authenticated, admin-authorized access."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The backend accepts JWTs with algorithm `none` and an empty signature, allowing any unauthenticated attacker to mint a token for an arbitrary identity — including the `admin` role — and receive full authenticated, admin-authorized access.\n\nComplete unauthenticated authentication bypass: the attacker impersonates any user or the administrator without knowing any credentials or possessing any key. Demonstrated as the seeded admin (full user list disclosure including all customers' emails and account metadata, cross-user basket access); the same forgery works against any user id for horizontal impersonation of orders, wallets, and personal data, and enables all admin write actions (product/price modification, order delivery status, accounting).\n\nPin the accepted JWT algorithms server-side to the expected signature scheme (RS256) and reject any token whose header declares `alg:none` or any unsigned variant; upgrade the JWT middleware/library to a version that refuses unsigned tokens by default. Additionally, never derive authorization decisions (role, user id, basket id) solely from token claims without validating signature and algorithm first.",
                "markdown": "The backend accepts JWTs with algorithm `none` and an empty signature, allowing any unauthenticated attacker to mint a token for an arbitrary identity — including the `admin` role — and receive full authenticated, admin-authorized access.\n\nComplete unauthenticated authentication bypass: the attacker impersonates any user or the administrator without knowing any credentials or possessing any key. Demonstrated as the seeded admin (full user list disclosure including all customers' emails and account metadata, cross-user basket access); the same forgery works against any user id for horizontal impersonation of orders, wallets, and personal data, and enables all admin write actions (product/price modification, order delivery status, accounting).\n\nPin the accepted JWT algorithms server-side to the expected signature scheme (RS256) and reject any token whose header declares `alg:none` or any unsigned variant; upgrade the JWT middleware/library to a version that refuses unsigned tokens by default. Additionally, never derive authorization decisions (role, user id, basket id) solely from token claims without validating signature and algorithm first."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.4",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0003",
              "name": "Unauthenticated user enumeration and security-question disclosure via /rest/user/security-question",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated user enumeration and security-question disclosure via /rest/user/security-question"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The unauthenticated security-question lookup endpoint discloses which email addresses are registered (full question object vs empty object) and returns the account's configured security question, providing a username-enumeration oracle and reconnaissance for targeted password-reset attacks."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The unauthenticated security-question lookup endpoint discloses which email addresses are registered (full question object vs empty object) and returns the account's configured security question, providing a username-enumeration oracle and reconnaissance for targeted password-reset attacks.\n\nAny anonymous attacker can reliably enumerate registered accounts and obtain each account's security question, focusing brute-force and phishing attacks on real users; the question text materially aids guessing the answer for the password-reset flow. On this instance, 28+ seeded accounts are confirmable within seconds. No direct data compromise beyond account existence and question disclosure was demonstrated.\n\nReturn a generic, identical response (e.g., an empty object or a neutral message) regardless of whether the email exists; never disclose the configured security question until the user has proven control of the account through a different channel (e.g., emailed reset link), and rate-limit the lookup.",
                "markdown": "The unauthenticated security-question lookup endpoint discloses which email addresses are registered (full question object vs empty object) and returns the account's configured security question, providing a username-enumeration oracle and reconnaissance for targeted password-reset attacks.\n\nAny anonymous attacker can reliably enumerate registered accounts and obtain each account's security question, focusing brute-force and phishing attacks on real users; the question text materially aids guessing the answer for the password-reset flow. On this instance, 28+ seeded accounts are confirmable within seconds. No direct data compromise beyond account existence and question disclosure was demonstrated.\n\nReturn a generic, identical response (e.g., an empty object or a neutral message) regardless of whether the email exists; never disclose the configured security question until the user has proven control of the account through a different channel (e.g., emailed reset link), and rate-limit the lookup."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-639",
              "name": "IDOR: Any authenticated user can read any other user's shopping basket (GET /rest/basket/:id)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IDOR: Any authenticated user can read any other user's shopping basket (GET /rest/basket/:id)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `GET /rest/basket/:id` endpoint returns any user's shopping basket to any authenticated customer. The server validates the JWT but never checks that the requested basket ID belongs to the token's user, allowing a logged-in customer to enumerate and read every other user's basket, including current product selections, quantities, and prices."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `GET /rest/basket/:id` endpoint returns any user's shopping basket to any authenticated customer. The server validates the JWT but never checks that the requested basket ID belongs to the token's user, allowing a logged-in customer to enumerate and read every other user's basket, including current product selections, quantities, and prices.\n\nAny registered customer can enumerate baskets 1..N and read every other user's shopping activity in real time: which products they intend to buy, quantities, and prices. This is a direct disclosure of other users' purchase-intent data (business-confidential in a real shop) with trivially guessable integer IDs.\n\nBind the basket lookup to the authenticated principal: after JWT validation, derive the basket ID from the token's `bid` claim (as the application already does for the caller's own basket) or, when an explicit ID is accepted, query with a `where` clause on both the requested basket ID and the token user's ID, returning a 403/404 on mismatch. Do not rely on the client to pass its own basket ID.",
                "markdown": "The `GET /rest/basket/:id` endpoint returns any user's shopping basket to any authenticated customer. The server validates the JWT but never checks that the requested basket ID belongs to the token's user, allowing a logged-in customer to enumerate and read every other user's basket, including current product selections, quantities, and prices.\n\nAny registered customer can enumerate baskets 1..N and read every other user's shopping activity in real time: which products they intend to buy, quantities, and prices. This is a direct disclosure of other users' purchase-intent data (business-confidential in a real shop) with trivially guessable integer IDs.\n\nBind the basket lookup to the authenticated principal: after JWT validation, derive the basket ID from the token's `bid` claim (as the application already does for the caller's own basket) or, when an explicit ID is accepted, query with a `where` clause on both the requested basket ID and the token user's ID, returning a 403/404 on mismatch. Do not rely on the client to pass its own basket ID."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-862",
              "name": "Broken access control: any customer can list all users' emails, roles and last-login IPs (GET /api/Users)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Broken access control: any customer can list all users' emails, roles and last-login IPs (GET /api/Users)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Any authenticated customer can retrieve the complete user directory: `GET /api/Users` returns every registered account's email, role, last-login IP, profile image and activity flags, and `GET /api/Users/:id` exposes any individual record. The endpoint requires authentication but performs no role check — a plain customer receives the admin account's record alongside all other users'."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Any authenticated customer can retrieve the complete user directory: `GET /api/Users` returns every registered account's email, role, last-login IP, profile image and activity flags, and `GET /api/Users/:id` exposes any individual record. The endpoint requires authentication but performs no role check — a plain customer receives the admin account's record alongside all other users'.\n\nAny customer obtains a complete mailing list of all users including the administrator's account identity (email + role), plus per-user last-login IP addresses — reconnaissance material for credential attacks, phishing, and targeted privilege-escalation attempts against the admin account. Enumeration of valid emails also feeds the password-reset and security-question flows.\n\nRestrict user-record reads to privileged roles: require an admin role claim in the JWT on `GET /api/Users` and `GET /api/Users/:id`, and return 403 for other roles. If customers legitimately need their own record, expose only `GET /api/Users/<own id>` resolved from the token, never a directory listing.",
                "markdown": "Any authenticated customer can retrieve the complete user directory: `GET /api/Users` returns every registered account's email, role, last-login IP, profile image and activity flags, and `GET /api/Users/:id` exposes any individual record. The endpoint requires authentication but performs no role check — a plain customer receives the admin account's record alongside all other users'.\n\nAny customer obtains a complete mailing list of all users including the administrator's account identity (email + role), plus per-user last-login IP addresses — reconnaissance material for credential attacks, phishing, and targeted privilege-escalation attempts against the admin account. Enumeration of valid emails also feeds the password-reset and security-question flows.\n\nRestrict user-record reads to privileged roles: require an admin role claim in the JWT on `GET /api/Users` and `GET /api/Users/:id`, and return 403 for other roles. If customers legitimately need their own record, expose only `GET /api/Users/<own id>` resolved from the token, never a directory listing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0010",
              "name": "Password change via GET with credentials in URL query string, response discloses password hash",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Password change via GET with credentials in URL query string, response discloses password hash"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The password-change operation is performed via `GET /rest/user/change-password` with the current and new passwords transmitted as URL query-string parameters, causing credentials to be stored in browser history, proxy logs, and server access logs. The successful response additionally returns the user's full record including the (unsalted MD5) password hash."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The password-change operation is performed via `GET /rest/user/change-password` with the current and new passwords transmitted as URL query-string parameters, causing credentials to be stored in browser history, proxy logs, and server access logs. The successful response additionally returns the user's full record including the (unsalted MD5) password hash.\n\nCurrent and new passwords persist in cleartext in URL-based log stores (browser history, proxies, referer headers, server access logs), which in a real deployment exposes user credentials to anyone with log access. The response hash disclosure (plus unsalted MD5 password storage) enables offline cracking of the user's password. No cross-user modification was demonstrated; the attacker must already hold a valid session token for this account.\n\nMove the password-change operation to a `POST`/`PUT` endpoint with credentials in the request body over TLS; never place passwords in URLs. Stop returning the password hash (or any password-derived value) in API responses, and replace unsalted MD5 password hashing with a dedicated password hash such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2.",
                "markdown": "The password-change operation is performed via `GET /rest/user/change-password` with the current and new passwords transmitted as URL query-string parameters, causing credentials to be stored in browser history, proxy logs, and server access logs. The successful response additionally returns the user's full record including the (unsalted MD5) password hash.\n\nCurrent and new passwords persist in cleartext in URL-based log stores (browser history, proxies, referer headers, server access logs), which in a real deployment exposes user credentials to anyone with log access. The response hash disclosure (plus unsalted MD5 password storage) enables offline cracking of the user's password. No cross-user modification was demonstrated; the attacker must already hold a valid session token for this account.\n\nMove the password-change operation to a `POST`/`PUT` endpoint with credentials in the request body over TLS; never place passwords in URLs. Stop returning the password hash (or any password-derived value) in API responses, and replace unsalted MD5 password hashing with a dedicated password hash such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0011",
              "name": "JWTs never expire: missing exp claim and no server-side lifetime validation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "JWTs never expire: missing exp claim and no server-side lifetime validation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Session JWTs contain no `exp` (or `nbf`) claim and the backend performs no lifetime validation, so issued tokens never expire and can be replayed indefinitely — including tokens whose `iat` dates back decades."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Session JWTs contain no `exp` (or `nbf`) claim and the backend performs no lifetime validation, so issued tokens never expire and can be replayed indefinitely — including tokens whose `iat` dates back decades.\n\nOnce a token is obtained (via XSS exfiltration, log/proxy capture, or the credential-in-URL exposure reported separately), the attacker retains permanent access to the victim account — password changes by the victim do not invalidate it. Combined with the token payload embedding the full user record (including password hash and totpSecret), a captured token never loses value.\n\nAdd a short `exp` claim to every issued JWT and enforce expiry (with a small clock-skew tolerance) in the verification middleware; implement logout-time revocation (e.g., a server-side denylist keyed on `jti`) so password changes and logouts invalidate outstanding sessions. Avoid embedding the full user record in the token payload.",
                "markdown": "Session JWTs contain no `exp` (or `nbf`) claim and the backend performs no lifetime validation, so issued tokens never expire and can be replayed indefinitely — including tokens whose `iat` dates back decades.\n\nOnce a token is obtained (via XSS exfiltration, log/proxy capture, or the credential-in-URL exposure reported separately), the attacker retains permanent access to the victim account — password changes by the victim do not invalidate it. Combined with the token payload embedding the full user record (including password hash and totpSecret), a captured token never loses value.\n\nAdd a short `exp` claim to every issued JWT and enforce expiry (with a small clock-skew tolerance) in the verification middleware; implement logout-time revocation (e.g., a server-side denylist keyed on `jti`) so password changes and logouts invalidate outstanding sessions. Avoid embedding the full user record in the token payload."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0012",
              "name": "Open redirect in /redirect via substring-based allowlist bypass",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Open redirect in /redirect via substring-based allowlist bypass"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `/redirect` endpoint validates the `to` parameter against a URL allowlist using a substring (`includes`) check instead of exact matching. Any URL that embeds an allowlisted string anywhere — for example as userinfo credentials — passes validation, producing a 302 redirect to an attacker-controlled host."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `/redirect` endpoint validates the `to` parameter against a URL allowlist using a substring (`includes`) check instead of exact matching. Any URL that embeds an allowlisted string anywhere — for example as userinfo credentials — passes validation, producing a 302 redirect to an attacker-controlled host.\n\nAn attacker can craft a link on the trusted application origin (e.g. `/redirect?to=https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop@evil.com`) that silently lands victims on an arbitrary external site, enabling credential phishing and token interception while the browser address bar initially shows the trusted origin. In this instance the impact is phishing/brand abuse only (no OAuth/redirect_uri flow was observed consuming this endpoint).\n\nReplace the substring check with exact-match validation: parse the `to` parameter with a single URL parser and require the full URL to exactly equal one allowlisted entry (or to match an exact scheme + host + path-prefix allowlist after canonicalization). Reject any URL containing userinfo (`@` in the authority component), and treat query/fragment differences as mismatches.",
                "markdown": "The `/redirect` endpoint validates the `to` parameter against a URL allowlist using a substring (`includes`) check instead of exact matching. Any URL that embeds an allowlisted string anywhere — for example as userinfo credentials — passes validation, producing a 302 redirect to an attacker-controlled host.\n\nAn attacker can craft a link on the trusted application origin (e.g. `/redirect?to=https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop@evil.com`) that silently lands victims on an arbitrary external site, enabling credential phishing and token interception while the browser address bar initially shows the trusted origin. In this instance the impact is phishing/brand abuse only (no OAuth/redirect_uri flow was observed consuming this endpoint).\n\nReplace the substring check with exact-match validation: parse the `to` parameter with a single URL parser and require the full URL to exactly equal one allowlisted entry (or to match an exact scheme + host + path-prefix allowlist after canonicalization). Reject any URL containing userinfo (`@` in the authority component), and treat query/fragment differences as mismatches."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-79",
              "name": "Reflected DOM XSS via q parameter on search results page",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Reflected DOM XSS via q parameter on search results page"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `q` query parameter on the search results page is passed unsanitized through `sanitizer.bypassSecurityTrustHtml()` and rendered via Angular `[innerHTML]`, allowing arbitrary script execution in any victim's browser when they follow an attacker-crafted link."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `q` query parameter on the search results page is passed unsanitized through `sanitizer.bypassSecurityTrustHtml()` and rendered via Angular `[innerHTML]`, allowing arbitrary script execution in any victim's browser when they follow an attacker-crafted link.\n\nAn attacker can craft a link that executes arbitrary JavaScript in any Juice Shop user's browser. Juice Shop stores its JWT in a `token` cookie readable via same-origin script, enabling session theft, account takeover, unauthorized purchases, and defacement of the victim's view. Requires the victim to click a link.\n\nRemove the `bypassSecurityTrustHtml` call on the search query value and render it as plain interpolated text instead of `[innerHTML]`, so Angular's default contextual escaping applies. If rich rendering is genuinely required, run the value through DOMPurify before binding, and add a Content-Security-Policy header without `unsafe-inline` as a second layer of defense.",
                "markdown": "The `q` query parameter on the search results page is passed unsanitized through `sanitizer.bypassSecurityTrustHtml()` and rendered via Angular `[innerHTML]`, allowing arbitrary script execution in any victim's browser when they follow an attacker-crafted link.\n\nAn attacker can craft a link that executes arbitrary JavaScript in any Juice Shop user's browser. Juice Shop stores its JWT in a `token` cookie readable via same-origin script, enabling session theft, account takeover, unauthorized purchases, and defacement of the victim's view. Requires the victim to click a link.\n\nRemove the `bypassSecurityTrustHtml` call on the search query value and render it as plain interpolated text instead of `[innerHTML]`, so Angular's default contextual escaping applies. If rich rendering is genuinely required, run the value through DOMPurify before binding, and add a Content-Security-Policy header without `unsafe-inline` as a second layer of defense."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.4",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-79",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0014",
              "name": "SSRF with response disclosure via /profile/image/url imageUrl fetch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SSRF with response disclosure via /profile/image/url imageUrl fetch"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The profile image URL upload feature accepts a user-supplied `imageUrl` and performs a server-side `fetch()` of that URL with no allowlist, scheme, or IP-range validation. Any authenticated user can make the application server issue HTTP requests to arbitrary internal or external destinations, and the fetched response body is persisted and retrievable."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The profile image URL upload feature accepts a user-supplied `imageUrl` and performs a server-side `fetch()` of that URL with no allowlist, scheme, or IP-range validation. Any authenticated user can make the application server issue HTTP requests to arbitrary internal or external destinations, and the fetched response body is persisted and retrievable.\n\nAn authenticated attacker can (1) reach internal-only services from the application server's network position (demonstrated against the app's own loopback API with content returned to the attacker), and (2) make the server fetch any external host, with the response body stored server-side and downloadable by the attacker. This enables internal network reconnaissance, access to services not exposed to clients, and potential cloud-metadata credential theft on cloud deployments.\n\nValidate the `imageUrl` server-side before fetching: resolve the hostname and reject loopback, link-local (169.254.0.0/16), and RFC1918/private ranges; restrict the scheme to https (or http plus an explicit origin allowlist); do not follow redirects; and enforce a response size and content-type allowlist. Alternatively, proxy image uploads through a dedicated egress-restricted fetcher and never persist raw response bodies to user-accessible assets.",
                "markdown": "The profile image URL upload feature accepts a user-supplied `imageUrl` and performs a server-side `fetch()` of that URL with no allowlist, scheme, or IP-range validation. Any authenticated user can make the application server issue HTTP requests to arbitrary internal or external destinations, and the fetched response body is persisted and retrievable.\n\nAn authenticated attacker can (1) reach internal-only services from the application server's network position (demonstrated against the app's own loopback API with content returned to the attacker), and (2) make the server fetch any external host, with the response body stored server-side and downloadable by the attacker. This enables internal network reconnaissance, access to services not exposed to clients, and potential cloud-metadata credential theft on cloud deployments.\n\nValidate the `imageUrl` server-side before fetching: resolve the hostname and reject loopback, link-local (169.254.0.0/16), and RFC1918/private ranges; restrict the scheme to https (or http plus an explicit origin allowlist); do not follow redirects; and enforce a response size and content-type allowlist. Alternatively, proxy image uploads through a dedicated egress-restricted fetcher and never persist raw response bodies to user-accessible assets."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.4",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0015",
              "name": "SQL Injection in POST /rest/user/login email parameter allows unauthenticated administrator login",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQL Injection in POST /rest/user/login email parameter allows unauthenticated administrator login"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The **`email`** parameter of the login endpoint is interpolated directly into a raw SQL query. Supplying `' OR 1=1--` (any password) returns a valid session for the first matching row — the administrator account — including its full JWT and password hash."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The **`email`** parameter of the login endpoint is interpolated directly into a raw SQL query. Supplying `' OR 1=1--` (any password) returns a valid session for the first matching row — the administrator account — including its full JWT and password hash.\n\nComplete authentication bypass without any credentials: the attacker is logged in as the administrator (user id 1) and receives a valid admin JWT. The JWT payload itself also discloses the admin's password hash and TOTP secret. Using the token, the admin-only API was accessed successfully (GET /api/Users returned the full user list). In a production analogue this grants full application administration and database write access.\n\nReplace the raw SQL string interpolation in the login query with Sequelize's parameterized query API (e.g. `sequelize.query` with bind parameters or `Model.findAll` with a where object), so the email value is bound as data rather than concatenated into SQL text. Reject or escape quote characters in the email field as defense in depth, and ensure the login query selects by exact email match rather than a concatenated fragment.",
                "markdown": "The **`email`** parameter of the login endpoint is interpolated directly into a raw SQL query. Supplying `' OR 1=1--` (any password) returns a valid session for the first matching row — the administrator account — including its full JWT and password hash.\n\nComplete authentication bypass without any credentials: the attacker is logged in as the administrator (user id 1) and receives a valid admin JWT. The JWT payload itself also discloses the admin's password hash and TOTP secret. Using the token, the admin-only API was accessed successfully (GET /api/Users returned the full user list). In a production analogue this grants full application administration and database write access.\n\nReplace the raw SQL string interpolation in the login query with Sequelize's parameterized query API (e.g. `sequelize.query` with bind parameters or `Model.findAll` with a where object), so the email value is bound as data rather than concatenated into SQL text. Reject or escape quote characters in the email field as defense in depth, and ensure the login query selects by exact email match rather than a concatenated fragment."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0016",
              "name": "Negative basket item quantity enables negative-total orders and wallet credit generation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Negative basket item quantity enables negative-total orders and wallet credit generation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The basket item quantity endpoint PUT /api/BasketItems/:id accepts negative quantities, producing negative basket totals. Checkout then processes the negative-total order, crediting the paying wallet instead of debiting it — a repeatable money-printing primitive for any authenticated customer."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The basket item quantity endpoint PUT /api/BasketItems/:id accepts negative quantities, producing negative basket totals. Checkout then processes the negative-total order, crediting the paying wallet instead of debiting it — a repeatable money-printing primitive for any authenticated customer.\n\nAny authenticated customer can create orders with arbitrary negative totals and, by paying with the wallet, permanently increase their wallet balance by the negated amount — infinitely repeatable. The order history stores negative-priced orders and negative quantities, corrupting accounting records. In a real store this is direct financial fraud: unlimited store credit generation and unbounded ledger corruption.\n\nEnforce a positive-integer minimum (>= 1) on basket item quantity on the server at PUT /api/BasketItems/:id and at POST /api/BasketItems, using the same server-side tamper guard already applied to warehouse quantities (PUT /api/Quantitys/:id). Recompute and validate the order total server-side at checkout, rejecting any order whose total is not a positive number before any payment or wallet mutation is applied.",
                "markdown": "The basket item quantity endpoint PUT /api/BasketItems/:id accepts negative quantities, producing negative basket totals. Checkout then processes the negative-total order, crediting the paying wallet instead of debiting it — a repeatable money-printing primitive for any authenticated customer.\n\nAny authenticated customer can create orders with arbitrary negative totals and, by paying with the wallet, permanently increase their wallet balance by the negated amount — infinitely repeatable. The order history stores negative-priced orders and negative quantities, corrupting accounting records. In a real store this is direct financial fraud: unlimited store credit generation and unbounded ledger corruption.\n\nEnforce a positive-integer minimum (>= 1) on basket item quantity on the server at PUT /api/BasketItems/:id and at POST /api/BasketItems, using the same server-side tamper guard already applied to warehouse quantities (PUT /api/Quantitys/:id). Recompute and validate the order total server-side at checkout, rejecting any order whose total is not a positive number before any payment or wallet mutation is applied."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0017",
              "name": "UNION-based SQL Injection in GET /rest/products/search q parameter leaks all user credentials",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "UNION-based SQL Injection in GET /rest/products/search q parameter leaks all user credentials"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The **`q`** parameter of the product search endpoint is concatenated into a raw SQL query on the backend. A UNION-based payload extracts arbitrary database content; all 28 users' emails and password hashes were dumped unauthenticated."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The **`q`** parameter of the product search endpoint is concatenated into a raw SQL query on the backend. A UNION-based payload extracts arbitrary database content; all 28 users' emails and password hashes were dumped unauthenticated.\n\nUnauthenticated full-database read access: the entire Users table (emails and MD5 password hashes for all 28 accounts, including the administrator) was extracted, along with the complete database schema via sqlite_master. No authentication or user interaction is required.\n\nReplace the raw SQL string concatenation used for search with Sequelize's parameterized query API (bound parameters or a where-clause object), never interpolating the q value into query text. Apply allowlist validation on the search term (e.g. rejecting quotes and SQL metacharacters) only as defense in depth, not as the primary control.",
                "markdown": "The **`q`** parameter of the product search endpoint is concatenated into a raw SQL query on the backend. A UNION-based payload extracts arbitrary database content; all 28 users' emails and password hashes were dumped unauthenticated.\n\nUnauthenticated full-database read access: the entire Users table (emails and MD5 password hashes for all 28 accounts, including the administrator) was extracted, along with the complete database schema via sqlite_master. No authentication or user interaction is required.\n\nReplace the raw SQL string concatenation used for search with Sequelize's parameterized query API (bound parameters or a where-clause object), never interpolating the q value into query text. Apply allowlist validation on the search term (e.g. rejecting quotes and SQL metacharacters) only as defense in depth, not as the primary control."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0018",
              "name": "Wallet top-up accepts arbitrary and negative deposit amounts (unlimited store credit)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Wallet top-up accepts arbitrary and negative deposit amounts (unlimited store credit)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The wallet top-up endpoint PUT /rest/wallet/balance treats the client-supplied \"balance\" field as a deposit amount and adds it to the current balance with no validation. A customer can supply an arbitrary positive amount (unlimited store credit with no real charge) or a negative amount, driving the wallet balance below zero."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The wallet top-up endpoint PUT /rest/wallet/balance treats the client-supplied \"balance\" field as a deposit amount and adds it to the current balance with no validation. A customer can supply an arbitrary positive amount (unlimited store credit with no real charge) or a negative amount, driving the wallet balance below zero.\n\nAny authenticated customer can grant themselves unlimited store credit (observed: 5000 credited with no charge to any funding source) and can drive their wallet balance arbitrarily negative. In a real deployment this is direct financial loss: the attacker orders merchandise against self-minted balance. The negative-balance direction also enables further negative-total/wallet interplay.\n\nValidate the deposit amount server-side at PUT /rest/wallet/balance: require a positive value, and enforce sane per-transaction and cumulative deposit limits. Do not trust the client-supplied \"balance\"/amount field as the deposit amount at all — derive the credited amount from a server-side payment-provider confirmation. Reject negative amounts explicitly before mutating the wallet.",
                "markdown": "The wallet top-up endpoint PUT /rest/wallet/balance treats the client-supplied \"balance\" field as a deposit amount and adds it to the current balance with no validation. A customer can supply an arbitrary positive amount (unlimited store credit with no real charge) or a negative amount, driving the wallet balance below zero.\n\nAny authenticated customer can grant themselves unlimited store credit (observed: 5000 credited with no charge to any funding source) and can drive their wallet balance arbitrarily negative. In a real deployment this is direct financial loss: the attacker orders merchandise against self-minted balance. The negative-balance direction also enables further negative-total/wallet interplay.\n\nValidate the deposit amount server-side at PUT /rest/wallet/balance: require a positive value, and enforce sane per-transaction and cumulative deposit limits. Do not trust the client-supplied \"balance\"/amount field as the deposit amount at all — derive the credited amount from a server-side payment-provider confirmation. Reject negative amounts explicitly before mutating the wallet."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
