              "method": "PUT",
              "cwe": "CWE-639",
              "impact": "Any customer can (a) surveil all users' shopping activity in real time (products and quantities), (b) silently change what another user's basket contains — quantities they never chose, up to per-product stock limits, directly affecting what the victim pays at checkout — and (c) empty another user's basket, destroying their work-in-progress shopping. Quantity manipulation also lets an attacker push a victim's order toward stock-limit failures.",
              "technical_analysis": "`POST /api/BasketItems` validates that the supplied `BasketId` belongs to the authenticated user (401 \"Invalid BasketId\" otherwise), but the collection listing and the update/destroy handlers operate on bare record IDs with no ownership join to the basket's `UserId`. The result is inconsistent enforcement across handlers of the same resource: create is guarded, while read (listing), update and delete are not. Basket-item IDs are sequential integers, and the unfiltered listing itself hands every item ID to the attacker.",
              "remediation_steps": "Extend the ownership validation already present on item creation to the other handlers: filter `GET /api/BasketItems` by the caller's basket ID, and before `PUT`/`DELETE /api/BasketItems/:id`, resolve the item joined with its basket and verify the basket's `UserId` equals the token user's ID, returning 403 on mismatch. Derive the caller's basket from the token rather than trusting client-supplied `BasketId`/item IDs.",
              "counterevidence": "Checked the create path: POST /api/BasketItems with a foreign BasketId is correctly rejected with 401 \"Invalid BasketId\" — so an ownership control exists and demonstrably runs on creation. Checked whether update/delete failures were stock-related: the 400 responses (\"out of stock\", \"only up to 5 items\") came from stock logic, and a within-stock quantity (1→4) succeeded with 200 while the victim's basket showed the new value — the mutation was applied for real. Deletion returned 200 twice and the victim's basket was emptied each time. The controls on create simply do not cover update/destroy or the listing.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If quantity manipulation could be shown to alter the victim's charged total at checkout beyond stock limits, or to let the attacker exceed per-user purchase limits at the victim's expense, integrity impact would rise toward High.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Register attacker A and victim B; obtain JWTs and B's basket ID (`bid` from login). 2. As B, add an in-stock product to B's basket. 3. As A, request `GET /api/BasketItems` — B's item is visible with its ID. 4. As A, send `PUT /api/BasketItems/<B's item id>` with `{\"quantity\": 4, \"ProductId\": <same>, \"BasketId\": <B's bid>}`. 5. As B, request `GET /rest/basket/<bid>` — quantity is now 4. 6. As A, send `DELETE /api/BasketItems/<B's item id>`; as B, verify the basket is empty. 7. Confirm create is guarded: `POST /api/BasketItems` with B's BasketId using A's token returns 401.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "vuln-0010",
          "ruleIndex": 5,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Password change via GET with credentials in URL query string, response discloses password hash\n\nThe password-change operation is performed via `GET /rest/user/change-password` with the current and new passwords transmitted as URL query-string parameters, causing credentials to be stored in browser history, proxy logs, and server access logs. The successful response additionally returns the user's full record including the (unsalted MD5) password hash."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "http://host.docker.internal:3001",
                  "kind": "resource"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "71a18e6651d0c08956389c1a7a42137f46eac2ecbd50b5a2554ee9926bbf3707"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "5424a1a5017a483f95a6a1005d533da360db58166e08eff16c0b5a77bd3d387b",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0010",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-11 19:59:30 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3001",
              "impact": "Current and new passwords persist in cleartext in URL-based log stores (browser history, proxies, referer headers, server access logs), which in a real deployment exposes user credentials to anyone with log access. The response hash disclosure (plus unsalted MD5 password storage) enables offline cracking of the user's password. No cross-user modification was demonstrated; the attacker must already hold a valid session token for this account.",
              "technical_analysis": "The Angular SPA calls `GET /rest/user/change-password?current=<pwd>&new=<pwd>&repeat=<pwd>` (visible in the main.js bundle). The handler verifies the current password, updates the record, and returns the full updated Sequelize user object — which includes the `password` attribute (an unsalted MD5 hex digest, confirmed by comparing the returned hash to `md5()` of the new password) — directly in the JSON response. Because it is a GET with a query string, both the old and new passwords are persisted in any URL-recording layer.",
              "remediation_steps": "Move the password-change operation to a `POST`/`PUT` endpoint with credentials in the request body over TLS; never place passwords in URLs. Stop returning the password hash (or any password-derived value) in API responses, and replace unsalted MD5 password hashing with a dedicated password hash such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively tested the CSRF angle: when the token is supplied via the `token` cookie (the browser-authenticatable channel), the endpoint refuses with `Error: Blocked illegal activity` and header IP spoofing (X-Forwarded-For, True-Client-IP, X-Origin-IP) does not bypass the block — so a cross-site CSRF attack via `<img src=...>` would fail on a cookie-authenticated session. The weakness that remains is the GET method with credentials in the URL and the verification being bypassable by attacker-controlled header/URL logging, not a direct CSRF exploit. Authorization is enforced (wrong current password returns 401; unauthenticated calls are blocked).",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Demonstrating log/history access capturing these URLs in a real deployment would confirm the credential-exposure impact and could raise severity; the hash-disclosure component would drop out if the response stopped embedding the user record.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Log in to obtain a valid JWT. 2. Send `GET /rest/user/change-password?current=<current>&new=<new>&repeat=<new>` with the token as `Authorization: Bearer`. 3. Observe a 200 response whose `user` object contains the account record including the new password hash (unsalted MD5). 4. Note that the credentials traveled fully in the request URL, which is recorded in browser/proxy/server logs.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "vuln-0011",
          "ruleIndex": 6,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "JWTs never expire: missing exp claim and no server-side lifetime validation\n\nSession JWTs contain no `exp` (or `nbf`) claim and the backend performs no lifetime validation, so issued tokens never expire and can be replayed indefinitely — including tokens whose `iat` dates back decades."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "http://host.docker.internal:3001",
                  "kind": "resource"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "e8ba4d59a5fa18c6658e3891e5f4720ca3d4ac47202c9c1eb394b37ef4479e7d"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "ce2b6c74d7aa42c8c94897408c35646a7d33f9d21baaea1e8c2c4e26b3426850",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0011",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-11 19:59:55 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3001",
              "impact": "Once a token is obtained (via XSS exfiltration, log/proxy capture, or the credential-in-URL exposure reported separately), the attacker retains permanent access to the victim account — password changes by the victim do not invalidate it. Combined with the token payload embedding the full user record (including password hash and totpSecret), a captured token never loses value.",
              "technical_analysis": "The JWT issued at login signs a payload of `{\"data\": <full user record>, \"bid\": <basket id>, \"iat\": <issued-at>}`. No `exp` claim is set and the verification middleware applies no maximum-age check, so token validity is bounded only by signature correctness, not time. Testing tokens with `iat` values of 0 and 1600000000 (both accepted as fully valid sessions at the time of testing, ~1789156000) confirms no lifetime validation occurs on any claim.",
              "remediation_steps": "Add a short `exp` claim to every issued JWT and enforce expiry (with a small clock-skew tolerance) in the verification middleware; implement logout-time revocation (e.g., a server-side denylist keyed on `jti`) so password changes and logouts invalidate outstanding sessions. Avoid embedding the full user record in the token payload.",
              "counterevidence": "The `iat` claim is present, so the tokens are self-describing as long-lived by design rather than mis-issued. Signature verification for signed tokens is otherwise intact (invalid RS256 signatures are rejected), so the weakness is specifically the absence of lifetime enforcement, not general verification failure. I could not observe token revocation/blacklist behavior directly; if a server-side revocation list existed it would partially mitigate persistence after logout, but no revocation mechanism is exposed in the API and the alg:none acceptance (reported separately) already defeats any claim-based session control.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirming a working logout/revocation mechanism (or its absence) would refine the persistence impact; absence of expiry combined with a confirmed token-theft vector in a real deployment would raise severity to high.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Decode any legitimate JWT issued by `POST /rest/user/login` — the payload contains `iat` but no `exp`. 2. Craft/obtain a token with an `iat` decades in the past (or simply retain any older token). 3. Present it to `GET /rest/user/whoami` as the `token` cookie — the server returns the authenticated user object regardless of token age. 4. Tokens issued at session start remain valid after arbitrarily long time gaps with no server-side invalidation.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "vuln-0012",
          "ruleIndex": 7,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Open redirect in /redirect via substring-based allowlist bypass\n\nThe `/redirect` endpoint validates the `to` parameter against a URL allowlist using a substring (`includes`) check instead of exact matching. Any URL that embeds an allowlisted string anywhere — for example as userinfo credentials — passes validation, producing a 302 redirect to an attacker-controlled host."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "http://host.docker.internal:3001",
                  "kind": "resource"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "2efcba5f7e181a570a2cc78853f5ce667b1b6ac6c02f4cc233f99652a89481db"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "80706fe9167a045bb171b04e2c0c9835ee2961b679abb457e0c1b45b32ca8195",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0012",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-11 20:00:12 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3001",
              "method": "GET",
              "impact": "An attacker can craft a link on the trusted application origin (e.g. `/redirect?to=https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop@evil.com`) that silently lands victims on an arbitrary external site, enabling credential phishing and token interception while the browser address bar initially shows the trusted origin. In this instance the impact is phishing/brand abuse only (no OAuth/redirect_uri flow was observed consuming this endpoint).",
              "technical_analysis": "The redirect handler resolves the target via an allowlist check implemented as `allowed = allowed || url.includes(allowedUrl)` over a fixed set of allowlisted URLs (e.g. `https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop`, `https://blockchain.info/address/1AbKfgvw9psQ41NbLi8kufDQTezwG8DRZm`). Because a substring anywhere in the candidate URL satisfies the check, the userinfo URL form `https://<allowlisted-url>@<attacker-host>` passes validation. The handler then issues `res.redirect(toUrl)`, emitting the attacker-controlled URL in the `Location` header. Browsers ignore the userinfo component when resolving the host, so a victim following the redirect lands on the attacker's server. Path-traversal suffixes and query/fragment suffixes on allowlisted entries are likewise accepted (the full string is reflected in `Location`), confirming the validator checks containment rather than equality.",
              "remediation_steps": "Replace the substring check with exact-match validation: parse the `to` parameter with a single URL parser and require the full URL to exactly equal one allowlisted entry (or to match an exact scheme + host + path-prefix allowlist after canonicalization). Reject any URL containing userinfo (`@` in the authority component), and treat query/fragment differences as mismatches.",
              "counterevidence": "Checked alternatives that fail: arbitrary unknown URLs (http://example.com) correctly return 406 \"Unrecognized target URL\"; protocol-relative `//evil.com`, backslash `/\\evil.com`, case tricks, scheme-less, and http-variant forms are all rejected; `javascript:` and `data:` schemes are rejected; subdomain trick (github.com/juice-shop.juice-shop.evil.com) rejected. No CSP or user-confirmation interstitial mitigates navigation to the redirected target. The only constraint found is the allowlist itself, and the substring-match flaw defeats it; no WAF or rate limiting observed on this endpoint.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Discovery that any OAuth/OIDC or post-login flow trusts this endpoint as its redirect target would raise severity; conversely no such flow was found (severity would drop to informational).",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Send `GET /redirect?to=https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop@evil.com` to the application. 2. Observe `HTTP/1.1 302 Found` with `Location: https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop@evil.com`. 3. Open the same URL in a browser: the userinfo portion is ignored for host resolution and the browser navigates to `evil.com`. 4. Any allowlisted entry works as the embedded string; e.g. `to=https://blockchain.info/address/1AbKfgvw9psQ41NbLi8kufDQTezwG8DRZm/../../../evil` also produces a 302 outside the allowlist intent. 5. Control case: `to=http://example.com` (no embedded allowlisted string) is correctly rejected with 406 \"Unrecognized target URL\".",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-79",
          "ruleIndex": 8,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Reflected DOM XSS via q parameter on search results page\n\nThe `q` query parameter on the search results page is passed unsanitized through `sanitizer.bypassSecurityTrustHtml()` and rendered via Angular `[innerHTML]`, allowing arbitrary script execution in any victim's browser when they follow an attacker-crafted link."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
