          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "191d458b8f00371cef9c9d2feebe238320756ac4ba3fe4ffe6695ddd871ef8d8"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.4",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "018a9e26fd2098caea9a34ef1e1b91ac816e5289096c1f916a93affe5ae55aba",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0014",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.4,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-11 20:00:31 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3001",
              "method": "POST",
              "impact": "An authenticated attacker can (1) reach internal-only services from the application server's network position (demonstrated against the app's own loopback API with content returned to the attacker), and (2) make the server fetch any external host, with the response body stored server-side and downloadable by the attacker. This enables internal network reconnaissance, access to services not exposed to clients, and potential cloud-metadata credential theft on cloud deployments.",
              "technical_analysis": "`POST /profile/image/url` (authenticated via the `token` cookie) takes `req.body.imageUrl` and, without any validation, calls `fetch(url)` server-side. On a 2xx response the body is streamed to `<user-id>.<ext>` under the public uploads directory and the user's `profileImage` is updated, making the fetched content downloadable at `/assets/public/images/uploads/<id>.<ext>`; on fetch failure the raw URL is stored instead. Because the URL is used verbatim, the server can be directed at loopback/internal addresses (demonstrated: `http://127.0.0.1:3000/...`) or any external host, and the response body is disclosed to the requesting user. Note the request body must reach the handler via a `multipart/form-data` field, since the global JSON/urlencoded body parsers mount after this route.",
              "remediation_steps": "Validate the `imageUrl` server-side before fetching: resolve the hostname and reject loopback, link-local (169.254.0.0/16), and RFC1918/private ranges; restrict the scheme to https (or http plus an explicit origin allowlist); do not follow redirects; and enforce a response size and content-type allowlist. Alternatively, proxy image uploads through a dedicated egress-restricted fetcher and never persist raw response bodies to user-accessible assets.",
              "counterevidence": "Tested and ruled out as false positives: unauthenticated requests are blocked (\"Blocked illegal activity\" — the token cookie is enforced, so this is not an unauthenticated SSRF); JSON and urlencoded bodies are parsed after this route mounts, so `{\"imageUrl\": ...}` JSON POSTs are silently ignored (no fetch occurs) — only multipart form-field delivery works; outbound fetch failures fall back to storing the raw URL as the profile image without any server request. No scheme allowlist, IP-range blocklist, or DNS pinning is applied to `imageUrl`; arbitrary schemes accepted by `fetch` (http/https at minimum) reach the sink. Cloud-metadata-style targets were not separately proven (169.254.169.254 not exercised); the captured callback plus loopback retrieval already demonstrate arbitrary attacker-chosen destinations.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Demonstrating a fetch of the cloud metadata endpoint (169.254.169.254) returning credentials would raise severity to high/critical; conversely, if the deployed environment has no reachable internal services and egress is blocked, severity would drop to medium/low.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Log in and obtain the session `token` cookie. 2. Start an HTTP listener reachable from the application server (here 192.168.215.4:3999). 3. Send `POST /profile/image/url` with `Content-Type: multipart/form-data` containing field `imageUrl=http://192.168.215.4:3999/ssrf-poc-final.png` and the `token` cookie. 4. Observe the inbound `GET /ssrf-poc-final.png` on the listener from the application server (source 192.168.215.3, `User-Agent: node`). 5. Fetch `GET /assets/public/images/uploads/25.png` and receive the exact bytes the listener served, proving the server stored the fetched response. 6. Repeat with `imageUrl=http://127.0.0.1:3000/rest/admin/application-version` and retrieve `{\"version\":\"20.2.0\"}` from `/assets/public/images/uploads/25.jpg`, proving internal loopback fetch with content disclosure.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "vuln-0015",
          "ruleIndex": 10,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "SQL Injection in POST /rest/user/login email parameter allows unauthenticated administrator login\n\nThe **`email`** parameter of the login endpoint is interpolated directly into a raw SQL query. Supplying `' OR 1=1--` (any password) returns a valid session for the first matching row — the administrator account — including its full JWT and password hash."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "http://host.docker.internal:3001",
                  "kind": "resource"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "6e5026eb02c3fb8766a98c05e4e5624199b541e5c3a40db51b8e7bb9bd4b3476"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "9.1",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "ebd34625b590b8bdb4156533b0642eda496b0c366788543acba99ee0af71a2a2",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0015",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 9.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-11 20:00:57 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3001",
              "method": "POST",
              "impact": "Complete authentication bypass without any credentials: the attacker is logged in as the administrator (user id 1) and receives a valid admin JWT. The JWT payload itself also discloses the admin's password hash and TOTP secret. Using the token, the admin-only API was accessed successfully (GET /api/Users returned the full user list). In a production analogue this grants full application administration and database write access.",
              "technical_analysis": "The login handler builds its SQL query via string concatenation of the user-supplied email, on a SQLite backend reached through Sequelize. A single quote in the email produces a 500 `SQLITE_ERROR: incomplete input` page (raw parser error), proving the value is interpolated into the query text. The payload `' OR 1=1--` closes the email literal, makes the WHERE clause a tautology, and comments out the password condition, so the query returns the first row of the Users table — the seeded administrator (`admin@juice-sh.op`, role `admin`). The response then issues the normal JWT for that account; because the JWT payload embeds the full user record, the response also discloses the admin password hash and TOTP secret. The password field itself is not injectable (uniform 401 for all payloads with a fixed bogus email), isolating the flaw to the email parameter.",
              "remediation_steps": "Replace the raw SQL string interpolation in the login query with Sequelize's parameterized query API (e.g. `sequelize.query` with bind parameters or `Model.findAll` with a where object), so the email value is bound as data rather than concatenated into SQL text. Reject or escape quote characters in the email field as defense in depth, and ensure the login query selects by exact email match rather than a concatenated fragment.",
              "counterevidence": "Password field is not injectable — 8 payload variants in the password (with a fixed bogus email) all returned uniform 401 \"Invalid email or password\", so the weakness is confined to the email parameter. The injected login is not a valid-credential login: password \"x\" is rejected for every probe/test email tested outside the injection. No WAF, input validation, or parameterization observed on the email field; the single-quote baseline returning a 500 SQLITE error confirms raw string interpolation into the query.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If the JWT issued after bypass were restricted to a low-privilege account (no admin row first), the impact would drop to medium; confirmation of writable SQL primitives (e.g. stacked queries) would raise availability impact.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Send a POST request to `/rest/user/login` with JSON body where the `email` field is `' OR 1=1--` and `password` is any string (e.g. `x`).\n2. Observe HTTP 200 with an `authentication.token` JWT for `admin@juice-sh.op` (role `admin`, user id 1).\n3. Decode the token (no signature verification needed) to confirm the embedded role and password hash.\n4. Use the token as `Authorization: Bearer` on the admin-only endpoint `GET /api/Users` and observe the full user list returned, confirming admin-level access.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "vuln-0016",
          "ruleIndex": 11,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Negative basket item quantity enables negative-total orders and wallet credit generation\n\nThe basket item quantity endpoint PUT /api/BasketItems/:id accepts negative quantities, producing negative basket totals. Checkout then processes the negative-total order, crediting the paying wallet instead of debiting it — a repeatable money-printing primitive for any authenticated customer."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "http://host.docker.internal:3001",
                  "kind": "resource"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "5dda8dbd067c8f0553dbed6eb45706aee6d04ff3799443622b77f2ff64595947"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "bb1415703cdc919d98801798a8605040b3b53c5bfcc315abc0413e35bc70976e",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0016",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-11 20:01:15 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3001",
              "method": "PUT",
              "impact": "Any authenticated customer can create orders with arbitrary negative totals and, by paying with the wallet, permanently increase their wallet balance by the negated amount — infinitely repeatable. The order history stores negative-priced orders and negative quantities, corrupting accounting records. In a real store this is direct financial fraud: unlimited store credit generation and unbounded ledger corruption.",
              "technical_analysis": "PUT /api/BasketItems/:id updates the basket item quantity without validating that it is a positive integer. The Angular UI clamps quantities client-side (`quantity < 1 ? 1 : quantity`), but the REST endpoint stores any integer, including negatives. The basket total is computed as price × quantity, so a quantity of -10 yields a total of -19.90. The checkout handler POST /rest/basket/:id/checkout accepts this negative total without re-validation, creates the order, and when paymentId is \"wallet\" applies the standard wallet deduction of the (negative) amount — i.e. it adds funds to the wallet. The stored order (GET /rest/track-order/:orderId) confirms totalPrice: -19.9 and quantity: -10, demonstrating durable state corruption of the order ledger and the wallet.",
              "remediation_steps": "Enforce a positive-integer minimum (>= 1) on basket item quantity on the server at PUT /api/BasketItems/:id and at POST /api/BasketItems, using the same server-side tamper guard already applied to warehouse quantities (PUT /api/Quantitys/:id). Recompute and validate the order total server-side at checkout, rejecting any order whose total is not a positive number before any payment or wallet mutation is applied.",
              "counterevidence": "Checked for guards on the quantity path: the UI clamps quantity to >=1 client-side (JS `s<1?1:s` in the Angular bundle), but the server-side PUT /api/BasketItems/:id performs no server-side re-validation — the negative value is stored and propagated into the order record. Warehouse inventory PUT /api/Quantitys IS protected (403 \"Malicious activity detected\"), confirming the app has a server-side tamper guard pattern for quantities but it is not applied to basket items. No WAF or request validation rejected the payloads.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If checkout additionally failed to re-validate inventory deduction for negative quantities in a production deployment (e.g. corrupting shared stock records), availability impact would raise this to high.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Authenticate as any registered customer and add a product to your basket via POST /api/BasketItems.\n2. PUT /api/BasketItems/{id} with {\"quantity\": -10} — the server stores the negative quantity (200).\n3. GET /rest/basket/{bid} shows a negative basket total (-19.90).\n4. Record wallet balance via GET /rest/wallet/balance.\n5. POST /rest/basket/{bid}/checkout with {\"couponData\": null, \"orderDetails\": {\"paymentId\": \"wallet\", \"addressId\": <yours>, \"deliveryMethodId\": <id>}}.\n6. Re-read the wallet: it has INCREASED by the negated total. The order record confirms totalPrice: -19.9 with quantity -10.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "vuln-0017",
          "ruleIndex": 12,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "UNION-based SQL Injection in GET /rest/products/search q parameter leaks all user credentials\n\nThe **`q`** parameter of the product search endpoint is concatenated into a raw SQL query on the backend. A UNION-based payload extracts arbitrary database content; all 28 users' emails and password hashes were dumped unauthenticated."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "http://host.docker.internal:3001",
                  "kind": "resource"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "df3cda84824c5c883a0f2105f87049b1cd555615decfd3aeae95ed72321576f4"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "3d1cb6aefa0cdf4247fe3d1a745159a11360ff6432415a582fdf5431f247719a",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0017",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-11 20:01:59 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3001",
              "method": "GET",
              "impact": "Unauthenticated full-database read access: the entire Users table (emails and MD5 password hashes for all 28 accounts, including the administrator) was extracted, along with the complete database schema via sqlite_master. No authentication or user interaction is required.",
              "technical_analysis": "The search handler interpolates the q value into a raw SQL LIKE query on SQLite (the double-paren `'))` prefix indicates the value lands inside a `LIKE('%...%')` nested in the query text). A single quote yields a raw `SQLITE_ERROR: incomplete input` 500 page, proving textual interpolation. The payload `juice')) UNION SELECT 11,22,33,44,55,66,77,88,99--` closes the LIKE, unions a 9-column SELECT matching the Products projection, and comments out the remainder; the SPA's JSON response renders the injected rows, allowing arbitrary table reads. Commas in the URL path fragment trigger an Express catch-all mis-dispatch (search must be sent as a URL-encoded query parameter, not path), which is a routing quirk, not a control. Extracting Users via `SELECT email||password FROM Users` demonstrates credential disclosure; sqlite_master extraction demonstrates full schema enumeration.",
              "remediation_steps": "Replace the raw SQL string concatenation used for search with Sequelize's parameterized query API (bound parameters or a where-clause object), never interpolating the q value into query text. Apply allowlist validation on the search term (e.g. rejecting quotes and SQL metacharacters) only as defense in depth, not as the primary control.",
              "counterevidence": "Control query q=juice returns only legitimate product rows and no credential data, so the leak is caused solely by the injected fragment. Parameterized variants would have returned 404/no rows; instead the injected UNION rows render with attacker-controlled column markers (id=11..99). No WAF or input validation observed on q.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If the search query were parameterized server-side, extraction would fail — no other condition materially lowers this; confirmation of stacked-query support would raise it further (write access).",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Send `GET /rest/products/search` with query parameter `q` set to `juice')) UNION SELECT 11,22,33,44,55,66,77,88,99--` and observe an injected marker row rendered in the JSON response.\n2. Send the same endpoint with `q` set to `juice')) UNION SELECT type,name,1,1,1,1,1,'table',sql FROM sqlite_master--` to dump the full schema.\n3. Send `q` set to `juice')) UNION SELECT email||password,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9 FROM Users--` to extract every user's email concatenated with their password hash; the credentials appear in the `id` field of the returned JSON rows.\n4. Compare with a control request `q=juice` which returns only product rows, confirming the leak is caused by the injected fragment.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "vuln-0018",
          "ruleIndex": 13,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Wallet top-up accepts arbitrary and negative deposit amounts (unlimited store credit)\n\nThe wallet top-up endpoint PUT /rest/wallet/balance treats the client-supplied \"balance\" field as a deposit amount and adds it to the current balance with no validation. A customer can supply an arbitrary positive amount (unlimited store credit with no real charge) or a negative amount, driving the wallet balance below zero."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "http://host.docker.internal:3001",
                  "kind": "resource"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "d83ab56b1f3a96a7024abd8b374c02df6c8c1c42e94a233ce1d0af558dd2da66"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "da6d0049a258884b1097857d1be44267b90881133361c61d5eea83bd649e16e9",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0018",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-11 20:02:11 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3001",
              "method": "PUT",
              "impact": "Any authenticated customer can grant themselves unlimited store credit (observed: 5000 credited with no charge to any funding source) and can drive their wallet balance arbitrarily negative. In a real deployment this is direct financial loss: the attacker orders merchandise against self-minted balance. The negative-balance direction also enables further negative-total/wallet interplay.",
              "technical_analysis": "PUT /rest/wallet/balance accepts {\"balance\": <number>, \"paymentId\": <card id>}. The handler resolves the saved card by id (rejecting unknown ids with 402 \"Payment not accepted\") but performs no validation on the amount: the supplied \"balance\" value is added to the current wallet balance. Positive values mint store credit with no corresponding real charge (the app has no payment provider — any well-formed saved card \"succeeds\"), and negative values are added as-is, allowing the balance to go below zero (observed -100, then -49 after a subsequent +51 order). The endpoint therefore lets any authenticated customer set an effectively arbitrary credit position.",
              "remediation_steps": "Validate the deposit amount server-side at PUT /rest/wallet/balance: require a positive value, and enforce sane per-transaction and cumulative deposit limits. Do not trust the client-supplied \"balance\"/amount field as the deposit amount at all — derive the credited amount from a server-side payment-provider confirmation. Reject negative amounts explicitly before mutating the wallet.",
              "counterevidence": "Checked whether deposits were validated server-side: positive deposits are always ADDED (PUT 50 -> 51, PUT 5000 -> 4951 from prior balances), proving the handler adds rather than sets, and it accepts negative values with no floor at zero. The 402 \"Payment not accepted\" path only triggers on missing/invalid paymentId, i.e. the guard validates card ownership, not amount. No WAF or amount validation rejected any request. The arbitrary positive top-up was verified three times with amounts (50, 5000) never charged to any real funding source.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that a real payment provider integration exists and charges the card would lower severity of the positive top-up leg; none is present in this deployment.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Register/login as any customer and save a payment card via POST /api/Cards (e.g. cardNum 4111111111111111, expMonth 1, expYear 2087).\n2. GET /rest/wallet/balance to record the starting balance.\n3. PUT /rest/wallet/balance with {\"balance\": 5000, \"paymentId\": <card id>} — responds 200 with data 5000.\n4. GET /rest/wallet/balance shows the starting balance PLUS 5000.\n5. Repeat with {\"balance\": -100, \"paymentId\": <card id>} — balance goes negative; no payment is ever actually processed.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
